  "G2542": {
    "orig": "Καισάρεια",
    "tran": "Kaisareia",
    "phon": "kahee-sar'-i-a",
    "thad": "Caesarea = \"severed\"\n\n1.  Caesarea of Philippi was situated at the foot of Lebanon near the\n    sources of the Jordan in Gaulanitis, and formerly called Paneas; but\n    afterward being rebuilt by Philip the tetrarch, it was called by him\n    Caesarea, in honour of Tiberias Caesar; subsequently called Neronias\n    by Agrippa II, in honour of Nero.\n2.  Caesarea of Palestine was built near the Mediterranean by Herod the\n    Great on the site of Strabo's Tower, between Joppa and Dora. It was\n    provided with a magnificent harbour and had conferred upon it the\n    name of Caesarea, in honour of Augustus. It was the residence of\n    Roman procurators, and the majority of its inhabitants were Greeks.",
    "orin": "from G2541",
    "tdnt": "None",
    "posp": "Proper Name Location",
    "sdef": "From G2541; Caesaria, the name of two places in\nPalestine: - Csarea.",
    "atra": "kaisareia as h",
    "shor": "Caesarea = \"severed\". Caesarea of Philippi was situated at the foot of Lebanon near the"
  },
